          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Night symbolically denotes spiritual and moral darkness—sin, unbelief, evil, and separation from divine light—while also commonly representing the fallen age before Christ’s kingdom, sleep or death, and concealed wrongdoing.\",\"book_summary\":\"Night is the absence of Day and Light, understood as visible obedience. It is characterized by sleep, death, and sinful deeds hidden in darkness, and at the broadest scale denotes the period from humanity’s fall until God’s kingdom begins on earth.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Night is spiritual darkness or disobedience opposed to God’s light and day.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Night is the absence of divine light/day manifested as the absence of visible obedience.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is evident. The book foregrounds sleep, death, concealed sin, and the fallen-age interpretation, all of which appear within the consensus responses as recognized applications or alternatives. It does not explicitly reject neutral or positive biblical uses of literal night.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book’s fallen-age chronology and emphasis on visible obedience specify familiar applications of the consensus darkness/day contrast rather than replacing it.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum cores, both readings identify Night with spiritual darkness or disobedience in opposition to divine Day and Light. The book’s larger-scale period from the fall until God’s kingdom corresponds to the consensus’s common eschatological reading of night as the present fallen age. Its associations with sleep, death, and hidden sinful conduct are likewise represented in the consensus and do not create a new or contradictory referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Genesis 1:5 and 1 Thessalonians 5:5–7 strongly support the day/night and light/darkness opposition and the association with sleep and drunkenness, but they do not by themselves establish death, concealed deeds, or the precise epoch from the fall until the earthly beginning of God’s kingdom.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
